let __v_547 = pyrt::literals::decode("{\"$dict\":[[\"ibuprofen\",\"Ibuprofen and alcohol both irritate the stomach lining, so one or two drinks with food is the sensible ceiling, and none at all if you have an ulcer or take a blood thinner. Paracetamol is the gentler painkiller for a big night. A single glass of wine with dinner is not the problem.\"],[\"paracetamol\",\"Paracetamol is fine with an ordinary drink - a glass of wine or a pint is not the problem. The liver danger is heavy drinking, or a dose taken while hungover when the last one is still in you, so never more than 4g in 24 hours and keep the drink light if you drink daily. Ibuprofen with alcohol is the one that upsets the stomach.\"],[\"antibiotics\",\"Most antibiotics are fine with alcohol. Metronidazole and tinidazole are the exceptions: they make you properly sick, so no drink until 48 hours after the last tablet. If the label says avoid alcohol, it means it.\"],[\"codeine\",\"Codeine and alcohol both slow you down, so keep it to nothing or one drink, and no driving or looking after a child on your own. Paracetamol or ibuprofen on their own are the safer pair with a drink.\"],[\"blood-pressure\",\"Blood pressure tablets and statins are usually fine with a drink in moderation: one or two, not a bottle. Alcohol lifts blood pressure a little for a few hours, so a heavy night shows on the readings even when the tablets are working. Grapefruit, not wine, is the thing to avoid with simvastatin and atorvastatin.\"],[\"antihistamine\",\"Antihistamines and alcohol both make you drowsy, and the older ones - Piriton, chlorphenamine - far more than a once-a-day cetirizine or loratadine. Keep it to one drink and don't drive on the pair; a non-drowsy tablet taken in the morning leaves the evening free.\"],[\"antidepressant\",\"Drinking on an SSRI such as sertraline is not forbidden, but alcohol blunts the good the tablet is doing: expect a lower mood and worse sleep for a day or two after. Keep it to one or two, and don't stack a sleeping tablet on top. On an older MAOI such as phenelzine, drink and cheese are both off the list.\"]]}")?;
