let __v_615 = Value::from("Hot flushes before 40 are usually not the menopause: an overactive thyroid is the commonest cause, and an infection, some medicines and low oestrogen from another cause do it too. The GP's first check is a thyroid blood test - and a pregnancy test before anything else if your periods have stopped. Note down how often they come and whether they bring a racing heart or weight change, because that is what tells the causes apart. See a GP if they are frequent, and the same day if you also have a fever or feel faint.");
frame.store("YOUNG_FLUSH_REPLY", __v_615.clone())?;
let __v_616 = Value::from("Hot flushes in midlife - most often the late forties and fifties - are usually perimenopause: keep the room cool, wear thin layers, cut back on alcohol and caffeine, and use a fan at night. HRT is the most effective treatment and is safe for most women - talk to a GP about your own risks, and mention any flushes with weight loss, a lump or bleeding after sex.");
frame.store("HOT_FLUSH_REPLY", __v_616.clone())?;
let __v_617 = Value::from("The first sign is usually the periods changing - shorter or longer gaps, then skipped ones - with hot flushes, night sweats, broken sleep and a low mood for most women. The average age of the last period is 51, and a GP can tell from your symptoms without a blood test. Ask about HRT: it is the most effective help for flushes, sleep and mood, and safe for most women.");
frame.store("MENOPAUSE_SIGNS_REPLY", __v_617.clone())?;
let __v_618 = Value::from("Flushes and night sweats last about four years on average, and around one in ten women has them for 12 years or more. The years of changing periods before the last one usually start in the mid-forties, the average age of the last period is 51, and symptoms that start early or last long are worth a GP visit - HRT shortens them for most women.");
frame.store("MENOPAUSE_HOW_LONG_REPLY", __v_618.clone())?;
